README-GCC -- using the gcc toolchains on this diskFour GNU C toolchains are here.  Each compiles, links and runs a Cprogram, with your own OS-9's assembler and linker and the setup below.WHICH ONE  Take GCC272 unless memory is short.  It is GNU C 2.7.2, the newest  here, it compiles C and C++, and its C++ is the only one newer than  1992.  Take GCC137 to read or rebuild the compiler you run.  It is GNU C  1.37.1, C only, the smallest of the four, and its whole OS-9 source is  here.  GCC139 (1.39) is the smallest C++.  GCC2 (2.5.6) and GCC139 have no  source here, and their C++ passes cannot be installed together.  Code an older one compiles, a newer one compiles too; not the reverse.THE FACTS  The C path is driver + preprocessor + compiler; the C++ path is C++  driver + preprocessor + C++ compiler.  `needs' is the Microware run-time  module used, as DOC/INDEX stars it; all of them ship in CMDS.                 version   C path   C++ path   source here   needs    GCC137       1.37.1     520 KB    (none)      whole       cio    GCC139       1.39       564 KB    891 KB      no          --    GCC2         2.5.6     1085 KB    922 KB      driver only cio    GCC272       2.7.2     1289 KB   1613 KB      most        csl (collect)  GCC2's C++ is gpp 1.40.3 running cc1plus 1.40.3; the 2.5.8 pass,  cc2plus, is in the directory but nothing forks it.  GCC139's C++ is  1.37.1.  Neither has templates or exceptions.  GCC137 and GCC272 have module names of their own (gcc137_cc1,  gcc272_cc2 and so on), so either installs beside anything.WHAT YOUR OWN OS-9 SUPPLIES  gcc compiles C to assembly, hands it to r68, and hands the objects to  l68 with clibn.l and cstart.r.  Those four are Microware's and come  from your SDK, as does `del', which gpp and GCC272 use to remove their  temporary files.  With your system on /h1, SYS/login makes r68, l68 and  del resident.  The as* programs here are cross-assemblers for the  6800 family and 6809, not the 68000.  Without `del', gpp ends with      gpp: Program del got fatal signal 221.  after your program is written.  The program is fine; the temporary  files stay.GCC272  /dd/CMDS/GCC272 holds gcc272 (C and C++) and its passes gcc272_cccp2,  gcc272_cc2, gcc272_cc2plus and gcc272_collect.  It forks the passes by  bare name, so point it at them with its -F option.  GCC2_OPTS carries  options for every run, and must be exported:      P=/dd/CMDS/GCC272      export GCC2_OPTS="-Fcccp2x[$P/gcc272_cccp2] -Fcc2x[$P/gcc272_cc2]        -Fcc2plusx[$P/gcc272_cc2plus] -Fcollectx[$P/gcc272_collect]"      gcc272 -uwlibs -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.c  -uwlibs links your own clibn.l.  The port's default C library, gclib,  is built partly from Microware's objects and is not here.  Its libgcc  is GNULIB/libgcc000.l, with 020 and 300 builds beside it for other  CPUs.  DOC/gcc272/readme.osk is the porter's guide.GCC137  /dd/CMDS/GCC137 holds gcc137 (C) and its passes gcc137_cccp and  gcc137_cc1.  -B names where they are, and the prefix includes the  start of the file name:      gcc137 -B/dd/CMDS/GCC137/gcc137_ -I/h1/DEFS        -L/dd/GCCLIB -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.c  It asks for a resident `cc1' and `cccp' before it looks where -B  points, and GCC139's passes carry those module names.  After using  GCC139, `unlink cc1 cccp' first.  DOC/gcc137 has the distribution's  documents.GCC2 AND GCC139  gcc2 (C) and gpp (C++) look for their passes only under two prefixes  compiled into them:      gcc2   /dd/CMDS/gcc_<pass>    and  /h0/CMDS/gcc_<pass>      gpp    /dd/CMDS/gpp_<pass>    and  /h0/CMDS/gpp_<pass>  GCC2's passes are installed there already, as byte copies of the ones  in /dd/CMDS/GCC2:      /dd/CMDS/gcc_cccp2      gcc2's preprocessor      /dd/CMDS/gcc_cc2        gcc2's C compiler      /dd/CMDS/gpp_cccp       the same preprocessor, under gpp's name      /dd/CMDS/gpp_cc1plus    gpp's C++ compiler      /dd/CMDS/gpp_collect    gpp's global-constructor collector  So, with clibn.l and cstart.r in /dd/LIB or /h0/LIB:      /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gcc2 -o hello hello.c      /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gpp  -o hello hello.cc  GCC139's passes are already named gcc_cc1, gcc_cccp, gcc_cc1plus and  gcc_collect.  Its gpp wants /dd/CMDS/gpp_cc1plus as GCC2's does, so  only one of the two C++ toolchains can be installed.  To use GCC139's,  copy its passes into /dd/CMDS in place of GCC2's.WHERE THE REST IS  /dd/GNULIB         os9lib.l, gnulib.l, gpp.l, libgpp.l, and GCC272's                     libgcc000.l, libgcc020.l and libgcc300.l  /dd/GCCLIB         GCC137's gnulib.l  /dd/GCCDEFS        GCC137's own headers  /dd/DEFS/GCC2      the gcc header set; its <stdio.h> forwards to                     DEFS/os9lib/stdio.h and adds SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR and                     SEEK_END  /dd/DEFS/os9lib    the C headers matching GNULIB/os9lib.lSOURCE  GCC is under the GNU General Public Licence, so this is what is here  and what is not.  Here:    SRC/gcc-1.37.1-osk  the whole of GCC137, as distributed with the                        binaries; README.OSK in it lists every change    SRC/gccsrc272       GCC272's source as released with it: driver,                        preprocessor, collector, libgcc and every                        target-independent pass.  It lacks the OS-9                        machine description and the C++ front end, so                        cc2 and cc2plus cannot be rebuilt from it alone;                        its README.OSK says what is missing    SRC/gcc142          the source of GCC2's `gcc' driver, which reports                        `gcc version 1.42'  Not found in any archive we could reach:    gcc 1.39 and its passes            CMDS/GCC139    g++ 1.40.3 (gpp, cc1plus)          CMDS/GCC2, CMDS/GCC139    gcc 2.5.6 (gcc2, cc2, cccp2)       CMDS/GCC2    cc2plus 2.5.8                      CMDS/GCC2  These came as binaries.  The 2.5.6 archive ships the GPL as `copying';  the 1.x archives carry no licence text.  Upstream GNU C of these  versions survives; the OS-9 ports' source is what is missing.  If you  have it, this collection would like it.